      "textEn": "275\n\nWhose; feet--feet; auspiciousness--auspicious; without hesitation--without hesitation; worshipers--worshipers; attracted--attracted;\nFes me ek yam si go rE ot kas pO\nTruthful body; three lives; exceeding--more than one; adorned beautifully; obtained ~ obtained; then--thereafter; glory--glory; people--people; he--they; me--me; abandoned--abandoned;\nExperiencing pride; at the end--at the end FL\n\nThe goddess of good fortune, Lakshmiji, For whose grace and blessings, even gods like Brahma yearned.\nAnd for whom they would surrender to God many times during the day, they, even after abandoning their residence in the lotus forest, were engaged in the service of the lotus feet of God.\nI had\nacquired special powers, from which I could, adorned with the flag, aw, goad, and lotus fod, which are the symbols of the lotus feet of God, could defeat the wealth of the three worlds.\nBut in the end, when I realized how fortunate I am, then God gave me\nsacrifice. Meaning: The beauty and opulence of the world can only increase by the grace of God, not by any human-made\nplanning. When Lord Krishna was present on this earth, the specific\nmarks of his lotus feet were inscribed on the dust, due to which the entire earth remained as full as possible.\nIn other words, rivers, seas, forests, mountains, and mines, which fulfill the needs of humans and animals,\nwere fulfilling their respective duties well. Therefore, the prosperity of the Earth\nwas greater than the prosperity of all the other planets of the universe.\nTherefore, man should ask God to always keep his grace upon him on earth, so that we may receive His unconditional grace,\nand we may have all the comforts of life, so that we may be happy. Someone might ask this question\nthat when, upon completing their work on Earth, God leaves Earth and goes to their abode, then\nhow can we stop them? The answer to this will be that there is no need for anyone to stop God. Because God is omnipresent, He can be present among us if we want Him to be. If we remain engaged in their devotion through listening, chanting, remembrance, etc., then they can always\nremain with us, because they are omnipresent."
